# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://json.schemastore.org/github-workflow.json
#
# E2E Workflow
# Runs Playwright E2E tests with a Neon database on the org's self-hosted runner pool.
#
# This file is copied to .github/workflows/e2e.yml by `pnpm setup:e2e`.
# Do not edit this template directly — it will be overwritten on next run.
#
# 🔴 WHERE THIS RUNS. `runs-on` targets the org's self-hosted pool
# (TimeKast/gh-runner-pool: labels self-hosted/e2e/playwright, 4 vCPU + 12 GB per job).
# Those runners ARE the Playwright image — browsers and system deps baked in — which is
# why there is no `container:` block here any more: nesting one would need
# Docker-in-Docker. The `playwright install` step below stays, and is what keeps this
# correct for every consumer: it installs the version THIS repo's lockfile pins, so the
# pool never has to guess (a version already baked into the image is a cache hit; one
# that is not downloads once).
#
# Every PRIVATE repo of the TimeKast org can use the pool as-is: the runner group
# (`e2e-pool`) is open to all of them, with no per-repo enrollment. Keep `runs-on` as it
# is and regenerate this file freely — there is nothing to re-apply afterwards.
# Only a repo OUTSIDE that set (public, or in another org) runs on GitHub-hosted, and
# then it needs TWO edits, not one: `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`, and
# `playwright install --with-deps chromium` — a hosted VM has no browser system libs
# baked in the way the pool image does. The pnpm cache below adapts on its own.
# Migration guide: .claude/docs/retrofits/self-hosted-e2e-pool.md
#
# Branch substitution: each `branches: [...]` line tagged with the trailing
# TRIGGER_BRANCH anchor comment is rewritten at copy time to the repo's RELEASE
# branch — its remote default, resolved from `origin/HEAD` — PLUS the working
# branch you ran the setup on, when it is a different one (see
# `formatTriggerBranches` in scripts/tools/setup-e2e.ts; fallback: 'main'). So a
# develop-first repo gets `[main, develop]` and a main-first one `[main]`.
# The literal value stays 'main' so a project that copies this template without
# running the setup script still gets a safe default.
#
# Required GitHub Actions secrets (configure under Settings → Secrets and
# variables → Actions, or let `pnpm setup:e2e` set them for you):
#   - DATABASE_URL    Neon connection string the runner injects into Next.js.
#   - NEON_API_KEY    Neon API key — the runner uses it to create an isolated
#                     database branch per CI run (account → API Keys).
#   - NEON_PROJECT_ID Neon project id the per-run branch is created under.
#
# Optional GitHub Actions VARIABLES (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions
# → Variables — not secrets):
#   - NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_*  The project's auth flags, as `pnpm setup:e2e` publishes
#                         them from `.env.local`. The step below is what puts them
#                         in the job's environment, and it filters by that same
#                         prefix. None published ⇒ the kit's defaults, unchanged.

name: E2E

on:
  push:
    # `pnpm setup:e2e` rewrites the anchored lines for you: the repo's release branch (its
    # remote default — `main` almost always, `master` where that is the convention) plus the
    # working branch you ran it on, when it differs. See the COSTO note above before widening
    # this on a repo that still runs on GitHub-hosted runners.
    branches: [main] # __TRIGGER_BRANCH__
    # Docs-only commits can't change E2E outcomes, and E2E is the priciest workflow here.
    # (Listed twice on purpose: GitHub Actions does NOT support YAML anchors/aliases.)
    # ⚠️ If E2E is a REQUIRED status check on PRs, a docs-only PR is skipped — which
    # GitHub reports as "not run", not "passed", and the PR stays blocked. Either drop
    # `paths-ignore` from `pull_request` or make the check non-required.
    paths-ignore: ['**/*.md', 'project/**', 'docs/**', '.claude/**']
  pull_request:
    branches: [main] # __TRIGGER_BRANCH__
    paths-ignore: ['**/*.md', 'project/**', 'docs/**', '.claude/**']
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      workers:
        description: 'Playwright workers (vacío = el default de playwright.config.ts)'
        required: false
        default: ''
      retries:
        description: 'Playwright retries (0 para cazar carreras: nada queda enmascarado)'
        required: false
        default: ''

# ⚠️ COSTO — por qué este workflow dispara donde dispara.
#
# E2E es, por mucho, el workflow más caro del kit: navegadores + build de producción +
# branch efímero de Neon, ~4-12 min por corrida. **En el pool self-hosted no consume
# minutos de GitHub** (cómputo propio, centavos por corrida), y por eso el default cubre
# la rama de release Y la de trabajo: es ahí donde aterriza el código, y verificarlo solo
# después del merge es verificarlo tarde.
#
# 🔴 Si tu repo NO está en el pool, ese cálculo se invierte: en runners de GitHub esta es
# la corrida que más minutos consume, y dispararla en cada push a la rama de trabajo se
# come la cuota mensual verificando algo que `/implement` ya corrió local y gratis en la
# máquina del dev (tk-implement Phase 4.1). En ese caso deja UNA rama en los `branches:`
# de abajo — la de release. Cómo entrar al pool:
# .claude/docs/retrofits/self-hosted-e2e-pool.md
#
# Ojo con `pull_request` en un repo que mergea `develop` → `main` SIN PR (lo que hace
# `/deploy`): ahí el evento nunca dispara y el único gate real es `push`. Si tu equipo sí
# abre PR hacia `main`, `pull_request` te da el mismo gate ANTES del merge, que es mejor —
# pero entonces `push` a `main` lo repite. Deja UNO de los dos según cómo mergees; tenerlos
# ambos paga dos veces por el mismo commit.

# Least privilege: without this block the job inherits the REPOSITORY default for
# `GITHUB_TOKEN`, which is `write` in many orgs. This workflow only reads the code and
# uploads a Playwright report — `actions/upload-artifact` authenticates with the runner's
# own artifact token, not with `GITHUB_TOKEN`, so it does not need contents write either.
permissions:
  contents: read

concurrency:
  group: e2e-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

jobs:
  e2e:
    name: E2E Tests
    # The org's self-hosted pool — open to every private repo of the org, no enrollment.
    # Public or outside the org? See the header: `ubuntu-latest` + `--with-deps`.
    runs-on: [self-hosted, e2e, playwright]
    # Cost guard: GitHub's default job timeout is 6 HOURS, all of it billable. E2E is the
    # most expensive workflow in the kit (Playwright container + a production build + a
    # Neon branch), and a hung browser is its most likely failure — exactly the case the
    # default would bill for 360 minutes. A full run is ~7-12 min; 30 is generous headroom.
    timeout-minutes: 30

    # Skip PRs from forks (no secrets access)
    if: ${{ github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.fork == false }}

    env:
      # V8 sizes its default old-space from the memory it can SEE, and inside the
      # Playwright container that default lands well under what the production `next build`
      # this suite runs needs — derived projects were hitting `JavaScript heap out of
      # memory` and patching the value by hand in every repo. One explicit ceiling instead.
      # Free in billed minutes: a ceiling reserves nothing, and an OOM re-run bills twice.
      #
      # Reaches the build and the Playwright process (the runner COMPOSES onto an inherited
      # NODE_OPTIONS), but NOT the per-phase `next start` — that one is set by
      # `serverEnvOverrides` in scripts/tools/e2e-runner.ts, which replaces the inherited
      # value on purpose. Raise it there, not here.
      NODE_OPTIONS: '--max-old-space-size=4096'
      AUTH_SECRET: ci-test-secret-minimum-32-characters-required
      AUTH_TRUST_HOST: "true"
      DATABASE_URL: ${{ secrets.DATABASE_URL }}
      NEON_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.NEON_API_KEY }}
      NEON_PROJECT_ID: ${{ secrets.NEON_PROJECT_ID }}
      # The branch the run's throwaway Neon branch is CLONED FROM — NOT the branch this
      # workflow fires on. CI fires on the release branch; the clone has to come from the
      # working branch, where the migrations of the commit under test live.
      #
      # 🔴 Without this line `resolveParentBranchName()` returns undefined under CI by rule:
      # the POST omits `parent_id` and Neon clones the project's DEFAULT branch, which in the
      # kit's dual-database model is `main` — production. The suite then runs against a stale
      # schema (every spec touching a new table dies with `relation ... does not exist`, which
      # reads like a product bug) on top of a full copy of production data.
      #
      # `pnpm setup:e2e` rewrites this value from the branch you run it on. Change it here if
      # you meant a different one — the setup warns before overwriting a value that differs.
      #
      # 🔴 Declared means enforced: if this branch does not resolve in Neon (missing, 403,
      # timeout), the run ABORTS before creating anything instead of cloning the default
      # branch. Remove the line only to knowingly accept the default as the parent.
      E2E_PARENT_BRANCH: develop # __E2E_PARENT_BRANCH__

    steps:
      # `persist-credentials: false`: by default checkout leaves the job token in
      # `.git/config` as an HTTP auth header, readable by EVERY later step of the job —
      # including `pnpm install` and `playwright install`, which run third-party code.
      # Nothing here pushes back to the repo, so the credential has no reason to survive.
      - uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          persist-credentials: false

      - uses: pnpm/action-setup@v5

      - uses: actions/setup-node@v6
        with:
          node-version: "22"
          # Only where it pays. On a GitHub-hosted runner every job starts on an empty
          # machine, so restoring the pnpm store beats fetching from the registry. On the
          # self-hosted pool the store already lives in the container between jobs, and
          # the round-trip to GitHub's cache cost 1.3 min per job — measured — for
          # something that was already on disk. An empty string disables the cache.
          cache: ${{ runner.environment == 'github-hosted' && 'pnpm' || '' }}

      - run: pnpm install --frozen-lockfile

      - run: pnpm exec playwright install chromium

      # Las decisiones del wizard de auth del proyecto (registro abierto/cerrado, contraseña,
      # passkey…) viven en `.env.local`, que es gitignored y nunca llega al runner. Sin este
      # paso, CI corre con los defaults del kit: `register.spec.ts` prueba el registro contra
      # una app que lo tiene cerrado, y pasa en verde.
      #
      # `pnpm setup:e2e` las publica como VARIABLES del repo (no secrets: son `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`,
      # o sea que `next build` ya las inlinea en el bundle del cliente). Este paso va ANTES de
      # `pnpm test:e2e` porque ahí adentro ocurre el build, que es cuando se hornean.
      #
      # 🔴 El filtro por prefijo se repite AQUÍ, del lado del consumidor: `toJSON(vars)` trae
      # TODA variable del repo, incluida cualquiera que alguien agregue después en la UI de
      # GitHub — y el runner spawnea con `{ ...process.env, ...overrides }`, así que un nombre
      # como `NODE_OPTIONS` o `DATABASE_URL` entraría al build y al proceso de tests. El filtro
      # vive en `selectCiEnv()` (scripts/tools/setup-e2e.ts) y NO en un `node -e` dentro de
      # este YAML: un control que nadie puede testear no es un control.
      #
      # `node` (vía tsx), no `jq`: `jq` no está garantizado en `mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:*`.
      # El JSON entra por `env:` y nunca interpolado en el `run`. La salida usa heredoc con
      # delimitador aleatorio, no `clave=valor`: un valor con salto de línea corrompería
      # $GITHUB_ENV y permitiría definir claves arbitrarias del job.
      #
      # Sin variables publicadas no escribe nada y el job sigue con los defaults del kit.
      - name: Volcar las variables de auth del repo al entorno del job
        env:
          E2E_REPO_VARIABLES: ${{ toJSON(vars) }}
        run: pnpm exec tsx scripts/tools/setup-e2e.ts --emit-ci-env >> "$GITHUB_ENV"

      - name: Run E2E tests
        env:
          # Empty unless a `workflow_dispatch` supplied them → `playwright.config.ts`
          # falls back to its own defaults. `E2E_RETRIES=0` is how a race is hunted:
          # a retry re-runs a whole worker, which is exactly what hides one.
          E2E_WORKERS: ${{ inputs.workers }}
          E2E_RETRIES: ${{ inputs.retries }}
        run: pnpm test:e2e

      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v7
        if: always()
        with:
          name: playwright-report
          path: playwright-report/
          retention-days: 7
